<DocsLayout title="Credentials Vault" activeNav="/platform/credentials-vault">
  <h1>Credentials Vault</h1>
  <p>
    The vault holds signing and publishing material for the whole workspace: keystores,
    distribution certificates, provisioning profiles, service account keys. Each credential has an
    encrypted payload of secure fields and, for the types that need one, an attached secure file.
  </p>

  <Screenshot
    src="credentials-vault"
    alt="Credentials Vault listing two credentials with type, secure field count, the app that uses them, and validity"
    caption="Credentials belong to the workspace and attach to build profiles."
  />

  <h2>Reading the list</h2>
  <p>
    Each row answers three questions: what it is, who uses it, and whether it is still good.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Column</th><th>What to look at</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Credential</td>
        <td>
          Name, type, how many secure fields it stores, and whether a secure file has been
          uploaded. <code>no secure file uploaded</code> on a keystore means the credential is
          incomplete and a build using it will fail.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Used by</td>
        <td>
          The application and identifier whose build profile this credential is attached to. A
          credential attached to nothing is dead weight, and the <strong>Unattached</strong> filter
          finds them.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Validity</td>
        <td>
          Expiry date, or <code>no expiry</code> for material that does not expire, such as an
          Android keystore.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>The status filters are the useful part</h3>
  <p>
    The pills across the top are counters and filters at once, and they are how you audit a
    workspace in a few seconds:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Filter</th><th>Finds</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Expired</td><td>Material that can no longer sign anything. Builds using it fail now.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Expiring</td><td>Material inside its warning window. This is the one to check before a release.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Unattached</td><td>Credentials no build profile uses — either stale, or a setup that was never finished.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>No file</td><td>Credentials whose secure file is missing.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>No expiry</td><td>Material with no expiry date, which is normal for keystores.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    A pill with a zero count is disabled rather than hidden, so the absence of expiring credentials
    is visible instead of merely implied.
  </p>

  <h2>Adding a credential</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Add credential</strong> asks for the type first, because the type decides the rest of
    the form: an Android keystore wants a store password, key alias and key password plus the
    <code>.jks</code> or <code>.p12</code> file; an iOS certificate wants a password and a
    <code>.p12</code>; a service account wants a JSON key.
  </p>
  <p>
    Secure fields are encrypted at rest and are never returned in a listing. Editing shows them as
    already-stored rather than displaying the value, and leaving a field blank keeps the current
    secret rather than clearing it.
  </p>

  <h3>Uniqueness checking</h3>
  <p>
    The workspace has a <strong>Credential uniqueness</strong> setting, on the
    <a href="/platform/team-access">Team access</a> screen. With it on, new credential forms look
    for an existing credential of the same type with the same file and identifying fields, and warn
    before you create a duplicate. Duplicated signing material is a real hazard: two keystores that
    differ only in name make it impossible to tell which one shipped a release.
  </p>

  <h2>Attaching to a build profile</h2>
  <p>
    A credential does nothing until it is attached to a build profile on an identifier. That link is
    made from the identifier — see
    <a href="/platform/applications">Applications → Identifiers</a> — and it is what decides which
    material a given build signs with.
  </p>
  <p>
    Because the vault is workspace-wide, one keystore can serve several apps. Deleting it affects
    every profile that references it, which is why deletion is refused while builds depend on it.
  </p>

  <h2>External vaults</h2>
  <p>
    A credential can also point at material held in an external secret manager instead of storing
    it here. The form then collects a provider and a reference rather than the secret itself, and the
    platform resolves it at build time. Verification reports whether the reference actually resolves,
    which is worth checking before a release rather than during one.
  </p>

  <h2>Doing the same from the CLI</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/cli/credentials"><code>appmctl credentials</code></a> covers listing, creating,
    rotating payloads, replacing files, and downloading material. Two notes carry over:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Secret payloads go through <code>--data-file</code>, never an inline argument, because process
      arguments are visible to other users and land in CI logs.
    </li>
    <li>
      Downloading material needs the <code>credentials:reveal</code> scope on top of read access —
      reading a keystore is a different act from listing that one exists.
    </li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
